WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.390
Our school's new tank alarm

2
00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:05.390
beeped six times on Monday, and

3
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.730
every time the tank was fine.

4
00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:12.010
By Wednesday, nobody even looked up.

5
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:15.600
On Thursday the tank really ran

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.980
dry, and nobody listened.

7
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.320
The rule was fine; the

8
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.140
number in it was wrong.

9
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.380
Today: choosing the moment a

10
00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:27.500
device should switch.

11
00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.190
The number in a rule is its threshold.

12
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.350
In fan on above thirty degrees,

13
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:36.110
thirty is the threshold.

14
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.160
On one side the device rests;

15
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:41.620
cross it, and the device acts.

16
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.080
First, look at real readings.

17
00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.350
On normal days the tank never fell

18
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.670
below thirty percent; when the pump

19
00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.430
broke, it dropped to twelve.

20
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.430
So the alarm belongs between the

21
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.030
two: about twenty percent.

22
00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.920
Any alarm can make two kinds of mistake.

23
00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.810
A false alarm: it goes off,

24
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.190
but nothing is wrong.

25
00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.760
A miss: something is wrong,

26
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.280
but it stays quiet.

27
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.590
Move the threshold to cut one, and you

28
00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.190
usually get more of the other.

29
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.760
Which costs more depends on the job.

30
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.580
A smoke alarm that misses a fire could

31
00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.060
cost lives, so it goes off easily.

32
00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.720
A spam filter is the opposite:

33
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.320
hiding a real message is worse

34
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.740
than letting spam through.

35
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.350
Quick check!

36
00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.050
A gas alarm in the school kitchen.

37
00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.990
Should it go off easily, or

38
00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.770
only when it's very sure?

39
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:40.530
Easily.

40
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.100
A missed gas leak could hurt people, so a

41
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.020
few false alarms are a fair price.

42
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.360
But keep them few, or people stop

43
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.180
listening, like our tank alarm.

44
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.970
Now a new problem.

45
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.470
A fan rule says on above thirty

46
00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:59.710
degrees, off below thirty.

47
00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.100
The room hovers right at thirty, so

48
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.440
the fan clicks on and off all

49
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.480
lesson, and wears itself out.

50
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.450
The fix is two thresholds with a gap.

51
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.850
Fan on above thirty degrees, but

52
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.630
off only below twenty-eight; in

53
00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:19.170
between, nothing changes.

54
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.980
A tank pump works the same way: on below

55
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.220
twenty percent, off above ninety.

56
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:27.010
Your turn.

57
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.000
A tank pump switches on below fifty

58
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.900
percent and off above fifty, and it

59
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.780
clicks on and off all day.

60
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.500
What's the best fix?

61
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.660
Two thresholds: on below twenty

62
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.800
percent, off above ninety, so the

63
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.560
pump fills the tank in one go.

64
00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.570
Remember: choose thresholds

65
00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:49.630
from real readings.

66
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.680
Ask which mistake costs more.

67
00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:55.110
And use two thresholds to

68
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:55.930
stop the flicker.

69
00:02:57.080 --> 00:03:00.240
Try it this week: find a device at

70
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.740
home that switches itself, like a

71
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:04.260
fridge or a water heater.

72
00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.230
Don't open it; just listen, and time

73
00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.430
how long it runs and rests.

74
00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.530
If it never flickers, it

75
00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.950
probably uses two thresholds.
